(>> 1.1 Foundations of Classical Mechanics and Idealizations) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Engineering Mechanics is the foundational physical science that predicts and describes) Tj
T*
(the state of rest or motion of bodies subjected to external force systems. In civil and) Tj
T*
(structural engineering, mechanics governs the design of bridges, multi-story frames,) Tj
T*
(dams, and retaining walls.) Tj
T*
(* Fundamental Idealizations: \(1\) Particle: A body of negligible dimensions whose mass) Tj
T*
(is concentrated at a single geometric point \(rotational effects neglected\). \(2\) Rigid) Tj
T*
(Body: A continuum of matter wherein relative distances between any two constituent) Tj
T*
(particles remain invariant under applied force systems \(deformations neglected in) Tj
T*
(rigid body statics\). \(3\) Concentrated Force: An idealization representing the action) Tj
T*
(of a force applied over an infinitesimal contact surface area.) Tj
T*
(* Newton's Laws of Motion: \(First Law\) A particle remains at rest or moves with) Tj
T*
(constant velocity in a straight line unless acted upon by an unbalanced resultant) Tj
T*
(force. \(Second Law\) The rate of change of linear momentum is proportional to the) Tj
T*
(resultant force and occurs along its line of action: F = m*a. \(Third Law\) To every) Tj
T*
(action, there is an equal and opposite reaction: F_AB = - F_BA.) Tj
T*
(* Principle of Transmissibility: A force acting on a rigid body may be transmitted) Tj
T*
(along its line of action to any other point on the same body without altering the) Tj
T*
(external external effects \(external equilibrium\). Note: Internal stress distributions) Tj
T*
(depend on the exact application point.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
