/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 1.7 University Review & Examination Questions) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Q1: State and prove Varignon's Theorem of Moments for a system of coplanar forces.) Tj
T*
(Answer: Varignon's Theorem states that the algebraic sum of the moments of a system of) Tj
T*
(coplanar forces about any point in their plane equals the moment of their resultant) Tj
T*
(about the same point. Proof: Consider concurrent forces F1, F2 acting at point P with) Tj
T*
(position vector r from origin O. The resultant is R = F1 + F2. Taking the cross product) Tj
T*
(of r with both sides: r x R = r x \(F1 + F2\) = \(r x F1\) + \(r x F2\). By definition, r x R) Tj
T*
(is the moment of the resultant M_O\(R\), while r x F1 and r x F2 are the moments of) Tj
T*
(individual forces M_O\(F1\) and M_O\(F2\). Thus, M_O\(R\) = Sigma M_O\(F\).) Tj
T*
(* Q2: Explain the physical significance of a Free Body Diagram \(FBD\) and enumerate) Tj
T*
(the systematic steps to construct it.) Tj
T*
(Answer: A Free Body Diagram is an analytical diagram of a structural body isolated from) Tj
T*
(its physical supports and adjacent elements, depicting all external active forces) Tj
T*
(\(applied loads, weight\) and reactive forces \(support reactions, cable tensions,) Tj
T*
(friction\). Steps: \(1\) Clearly identify and isolate the body of interest. \(2\) Replace all) Tj
T*
(physical supports with equivalent reactive force/moment vectors according to their) Tj
T*
(kinematic constraints. \(3\) Indicate all applied external loads and self-weight acting at) Tj
T*
(the centre of gravity. \(4\) Label all unknown coordinates, inclination angles, and) Tj
T*
(coordinate axes.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 1.8 Key Takeaways and Revision Points) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Concurrent forces meet at a common point; non-concurrent force systems create) Tj
T*
(rotational moments requiring moment equilibrium.) Tj
T*
(* Lami's theorem applies strictly to THREE concurrent coplanar forces in equilibrium.) Tj
T*
(* A couple consists of two equal and opposite parallel forces producing pure rotation) Tj
T*
